---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro';
import RefEntries from '../../components/RefEntries.astro';
import { pyGroups, pyEntries } from '../../data/pytools';
---

<Base title="Python tools reference" description="The Python features, matplotlib plots and pandas tables the notes' code uses, each in plain words with a runnable example.">
  <div class="note-layout">
    <article class="prose">
      <p class="eyebrow">Reference · {pyEntries.length} entries</p>
      <h1>Python, plotting and pandas</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        The pieces of Python the notes’ code uses that go beyond the basics, plus the few plotting and table functions
        that appear. Click any of them inside a code block to land here.
      </p>
      <Callout kind="note" label="What the notes assume">
        <p>
          You can write basic Python: variables, <code>if</code>, <code>for</code>, functions, lists, dictionaries and
          simple f-strings such as <code>f"{'{'}x{'}'}"</code>. Everything else used in the code is explained on this page,
          on the <a href="/ai/numpy/">NumPy page</a> or the <a href="/ai/sklearn/">scikit-learn page</a>, and in a
          “New in this code” box right above the first code block that uses it.
        </p>
      </Callout>
      <RefEntries groups={pyGroups} entries={pyEntries} />
    </article>
    <aside class="note-toc" aria-label="On this page">
      <strong>On this page</strong>
      {pyGroups.map((g) => <a href={`#g-${g.id}`}>{g.title}</a>)}
    </aside>
  </div>
</Base>
